Plan (signup endpoint)

1) src/api/account.ts - add POST /api/signup route
- Imports: add `createUser`, `UserError` from "../auth/users.ts" and `createSession` from "../auth/session.ts" (or import as in routes.ts). Also use `field`, `json`, `type Route`, `publicUser` (already there).
- Add new route object in `accountRoutes` array (order doesn't matter much; keep consistent with existing style):
  - method: "POST", path: "/api/signup"
  - handler: reads body fields email, name, password (use field). Calls createUser(email, name, password). On success, creates session via createSession(user.id), responds 201 with { token, user: publicUser(user) }. If createUser throws UserError, respond 400 { error: message } (catch and map). Missing required fields? createUser will likely throw; but task states body is {email, name, password} - we can let createUser validate or validate explicitly. But existing login explicitly validates presence and returns 400. Follow similar defensive style: if any field missing, return 400 with a clear error.

Exact code for the route:
```ts
{
  method: "POST",
  path: "/api/signup",
  handler: (req) => {
    const email = field(req.body, "email");
    const name = field(req.body, "name");
    const password = field(req.body, "password");
    if (!email || !name || !password) return json(400, { error: "email, name, and password are required" });
    try {
      const user = createUser(email, name, password);
      const session = createSession(user.id);
      return json(201, { token: session.token, user: publicUser(user) });
    } catch (e) {
      if (e instanceof UserError) return json(400, { error: e.message });
      throw e;
    }
  },
},
```

Also update imports in account.ts:
```ts
import { createUser, getUser, publicUser, UserError } from "../auth/users.ts";
import { createSession, revokeSession } from "../auth/session.ts";
```

2) src/api/account.ts - place the new route in accountRoutes array. Put it before/after others; appending is fine.

3) test/signup.test.ts - new file. Create tests covering the task requirements. Follow style of api.test.ts (use node:test, assert, app wrapper).
Suggested tests:
- successful signup returns 201 with token and public user; can use token for /api/me
- UserError cases return 400 with error (invalid email, short password, duplicate email)
- missing fields return 400
Base on createUser behavior: invalid email -> "invalid email", password < 8 -> "password must be at least 8 characters", duplicate -> "email already registered".

Example structure:
```ts
import { test, beforeEach } from "node:test";
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { app } from "../src/api/routes.ts";
import { resetUsers } from "../src/auth/users.ts";
import { resetSessions } from "../src/auth/session.ts";

const call = (method: string, path: string, body?: unknown, token?: string) =>
  app({ method, path, body, headers: token ? { authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } : {} });

beforeEach(() => {
  resetUsers();
  resetSessions();
});

test("signup creates user and signs in, returns 201 with token and public user", async () => {
  const res = await call("POST", "/api/signup", { email: "test@example.com", name: "Test", password: "password123" });
  assert.equal(res.status, 201);
  const body = res.body as { token: string; user: { id: string; email: string; name: string } };
  assert.ok(body.token);
  assert.deepEqual(body.user, { id: body.user.id, email: "test@example.com", name: "Test" });
  const me = await call("GET", "/api/me", undefined, body.token);
  assert.deepEqual(me.body, body.user);
});

test("signup returns 400 on invalid email", async () => {
  const res = await call("POST", "/api/signup", { email: "bad", name: "T", password: "password123" });
  assert.equal(res.status, 400);
  assert.deepEqual(res.body, { error: "invalid email" });
});

test("signup returns 400 on short password", async () => {
  const res = await call("POST", "/api/signup", { email: "t@e.com", name: "T", password: "short" });
  assert.equal(res.status, 400);
  assert.deepEqual(res.body, { error: "password must be at least 8 characters" });
});

test("signup returns 400 on duplicate email", async () => {
  await call("POST", "/api/signup", { email: "t@e.com", name: "T1", password: "password123" });
  const res = await call("POST", "/api/signup", { email: "T@E.com", name: "T2", password: "password456" });
  assert.equal(res.status, 400);
  assert.deepEqual(res.body, { error: "email already registered" });
});

test("signup returns 400 on missing fields", async () => {
  const res = await call("POST", "/api/signup", { email: "t@e.com", name: "T" });
  assert.equal(res.status, 400);
  assert.ok("error" in (res.body as any));
});
```

Notes:
- Route registration: accountRoutes are spread into routes in src/api/routes.ts (already done), so adding to account.ts is sufficient.
- Response code: task says "responding 201 with {token, user: publicUser(user)}" - 201 is correct for resource creation.
- Sessions: starting session same as login means call createSession(user.id) and return token (same as login returns { token }). Yes.

This plan matches the existing codebase style and test patterns exactly.
